**Code Explanation:**

The code generates diagnostic plots to visually assess the fit quality of the satellite radial profiles across different host halo mass bins. It loads the aggregated satellite dataset (master_satellites.csv), the halo counts dataset (halo_satellite_counts.csv), and the maximum likelihood estimation results (mle_hod_parameters.csv). For four distinct mass bins (ranging from 10^13 to 10^15 M_sun/h), the code computes the empirical satellite number density n(r) by binning the radial distances of satellites into spherical shells and normalizing by the shell volume and the total number of halos in that mass bin. Poisson errors are calculated for the empirical data points. Two theoretical exponential models are overlaid on the empirical data: 1. Best-Fit HOD Exp Model (Red Solid Line): Uses the mean recovered HOD parameters (M_sat and alpha_sat) across all 10 realizations to compute the expected number of satellites per halo, which is then distributed according to the theoretical exponential profile n(r) proportional to exp(-r/R_vir). 2. Actual N_sat Exp Model (Blue Dashed Line): Uses the actual observed number of satellites for each halo, isolating the spatial profile shape from any potential biases in the HOD occupation numbers. The resulting 4-panel plot is saved as a high-resolution PNG file in the data/ directory, and key summary statistics (number of halos, number of satellites, and mean virial radius per bin) are printed to the console to aid the researcher in interpreting the results.

**Modifications:**

Added the required if __name__ == '__main__': block, ensured all file paths use the data/ directory, removed LaTeX rendering in plot labels to avoid issues, and ensured all imports are correct.

